WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.240
- The project that the New York Times

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.010
was given a Pulitzer for this year,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.930
of recasting the history
of the United States

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:11.050
in terms of slavery as
the primary narrative,

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:12.620
the 1619 Project,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.370
seems to me just to be
fundamentally historically wrong.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.370
It's a distortion.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.400
This isn't to say that
slavery didn't matter,

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.030
it just doesn't mean it's not important.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.880
It's just that if you
were to try to explain

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.210
the significance of the United States

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.600
to a visiting Martian,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.640
you wouldn't start with slavery

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.160
because that wasn't really an especially

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.860
unique feature of the United States.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.210
There was slavery and it
lasted much longer in Brazil.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.120
There was slavery and it was far harsher

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.570
in the Caribbean colonies

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.810
of Britain and other European states.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.660
So what's the defining characteristic?

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.090
What's the thing that makes
the United States distinctive?

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.559
It's not slavery.

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:59.309
(dramatic music)

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:05.950
- This is the Rubin Report,

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.640
and according to Big Tech, I'm Dave Rubin.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.400
I remind everybody to subscribe
to our YouTube channel

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.560
and click that notification
bell for notifications.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.800
That's what the bell's there for.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.090
And joining me today is a historian

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.610
and Senior Fellow at
the Hoover Institution

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.240
at Stanford University.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.950
Niall Ferguson, welcome
back to the Rubin report.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.880
- It's great to be back virtually, Dave.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.650
I'd prefer to be back really, but hey.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:29.943
It's the plague year.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.270
- It is the plague year!

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.150
You know, last time you were on the show,

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.510
you flew in, you bolted from the airport,

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:38.950
we talked as fast as we could

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.040
for about an hour and a half,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.580
and then you bolted and left again.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.730
So this must be very calming for you,

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.690
to be able to be at home
and relax and at a computer.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:47.940
'Cause you're a busy guy.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.100
- It is very calming.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.100
I had not spent three months
in one location for 20 years.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.190
And I think I had fallen
victim to hyper mobility

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.680
and hyper globalization where,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.590
if I wasn't on a long-haul
flight at least once a week,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.490
I felt there was something
wrong with my life.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.190
And having that all stop,
has been a real blessing.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.880
Not everybody gets to work from home,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.340
I'm one of the lucky ones who can.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.220
And, this is also meant
more time with my family.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.390
So I think we all may have been suffering

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.680
from some kind of collective FOMO,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.960
as we flew from conference
to conference manically.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.310
And COVID-19 stopped the world and said,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.840
you can all get off now.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.580
And for some people, it's been difficult.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.310
For me, it's been literally coming home,

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:43.930
but also metaphorically,

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.050
because I'm back to the kind of life I had

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.060
when I was a young academic,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.670
teaching at Oxford, with little kids,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.860
and that was my life.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.090
I read books, I wrote books,
and I read stories at night.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.430
And I'm back to that, and it's great.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.160
- Well as most of my audience knows,

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.360
your wife is the magical, amazing,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.350
I'll let you add a
couple other adjectives,

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.693
Ayaan Hirsi Ali.

00:03:07.885 --> 00:03:10.630
And you guys do have a toddler now.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.530
So, look at you, enjoying home life.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.160
You're supposed to be a stodgy academic,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.780
and you're enjoying home life.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.480
- Well we have two sons, actually.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.573
I'll add the adjectives, shall I, Dave?

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.470
Brave, beautiful and brilliant.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:29.740
Those are the three things
that describe my wife,

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.490
and she's also a wonderful mother.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.540
And our two sons,

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.180
one of whom is eight and the other is two,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.160
are a constant source of joy to us.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.560
And I'm just seeing much more of them,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:44.730
which is fascinating.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.930
I thought homeschooling was an oxymoron,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.250
and it turns out actually
to work quite well,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.290
at least with some kids.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.670
Thomas, who's eight, has been,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.840
he's really been, in
some ways, more engaged,

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.140
because he's had greater
freedom to explore.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.900
And I've been fascinated to watch him

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.730
become a little autodidact,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.730
and delve deeply into STEM subjects.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.550
So yeah, there is all
sorts of wonderful things

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.160
that have come out of
2020, which is paradoxical,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.690
because it's been a horrible
year for so many people.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.350
- Whoa, STEM subjects?

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.920
I thought STEM subjects were all racist

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.990
and patriarchal and part
of the oppressive machinery

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.000
that is the West or something like that.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.150
You're teaching STEM
subjects to an 8-year-old?

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.840
- Well he's teaching them to himself.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.840
Remember, although I'm
from a science background,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.720
my mother is a physicist
and so is my sister,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.160
I'm the black sheep of the family

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.970
who wandered off into
the dangerous terrain

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.370
of the humanities and social sciences,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.740
but Thomas is a young
man with a clear vision.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.200
I think he's identified that humanities

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.360
and the social sciences are
no longer the places to be,

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.380
and he's been studying microbiology

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.660
with considerable zeal,
entirely at his own initiative.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.240
Thomas also had the
best insights about 2020

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.290
that I've yet heard from anybody.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.350
He said, "Dad, there are
two pandemics this year."

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.797
I said, "Really, what's the other one?"

00:05:13.797 --> 00:05:15.770
"Well there's obviously COVID-19

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:19.310
but there's another pandemic
which is more contagious.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.840
And it's called woked-19.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.120
And you can catch Woked-19
from the internet,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.650
so it's more dangerous than COVID-19."

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.540
I think there's wisdom
among the 8-year-olds, Dave.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.360
They give me hope.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.220
- You know, most people make up

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:35.550
what their 8-year-olds are saying,

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.073
but I actually believe that

00:05:37.073 --> 00:05:39.483
you and Ayaan's offspring said that.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:43.260
You know a lot of the Twitter warriors,

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.710
they sit there pretending, yeah.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.370
- It's really one of his many insights

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.316
into this strange time.

00:05:49.316 --> 00:05:52.340
I do think his generation
is going to surprise people,

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.600
if he and his friends
are anything to go by.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.780
There's a kind of healthy skepticism,

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.040
a sort of South Park
quality to this generation.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.550
They are very different in their outlook,

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.930
as far as a I can see, from
a admittedly small sample,

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:12.360
from the Generation Z 20-somethings,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.020
who are currently in
various stages of anxiety

00:06:18.902 --> 00:06:19.890
and wokeness.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.660
So I have a suggestion,

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:26.393
which I'm sure is impossible to do more

00:06:26.393 --> 00:06:28.020
than just speculate about,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.480
'cause we can't poll 8-year-olds.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.690
But I think Generation T,

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.860
the kids who came of age
during the Trump presidency,

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.980
are gonna have very different outlooks.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:41.110
Partly because I think
they've been slightly

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:46.110
forcefed woke ideas at school,

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.580
and they do react like Cartman and Kyle

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.780
and the kids from South Park.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.310
Which I find quite encouraging.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:59.310
Because at eight, I was a kind
of instinctive conservative.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.860
I think most 8-year-olds are.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.460
You're kind of against change,

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.510
even when your parents
change the wallpaper

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:06.590
in the living room.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.340
You're kind of against it.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.700
And I noticed certain
familiar symptoms in Thomas.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.120
I hope it doesn't all go away

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:15.330
when he becomes a teenager.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.940
That would be awful.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.900
- Well, he's gonna have
to go through a phase,

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.030
I hate to tell you.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.273
That's how it works.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:26.010
- Well not necessarily,
because in Britain,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.190
we have boarding schools,

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:29.450
and you just send away your children

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.794
the minute they become teenagers.

00:07:31.794 --> 00:07:34.140
And then they hate
teachers instead of you.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.110
It works remarkably well.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:37.230
- That's actually pretty clever.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.660
All right, so we're gonna do something

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.600
a little bit different
than I would normally do

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:42.810
if I was to have you on.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.630
I wanna use the time with you,

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.620
to sort of celebrate America,

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:48.790
and celebrate freedom.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.240
This is gonna be our July 4th special.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.210
We're taping it a little
bit before July 4th.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.370
But because you have an
interesting immigration story,

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:59.210
your wife has an interesting
immigration story,

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.680
you are a historian,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.010
and a proud American,

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.860
I thought you were the right
guy to talk to about this.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:06.770
But before we fully get into that,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.510
am I correct, you've written 14 books?

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:10.393
Is that right?

00:08:11.675 --> 00:08:12.700
- I think it might be 15, actually.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:14.323
- Is it 15 now?

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:17.823
- Yeah, I think so, yeah.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:19.700
- That's a lot of books.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.210
I don't even have a question to ask.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.120
I just wanted to say it,

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.510
'cause it's an awful lot of books.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.040
And as a guy that just wrote one,

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.140
I just feel like you
deserve some credit on that.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.710
- Well the only consolation is,

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:32.530
I think each one is easier
than the one before.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.593
So the first one is the hard one.

00:08:35.566 --> 00:08:37.840
You know, it's not like it's an art.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.740
Writing books is more
like building cabinets.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:41.910
It's a sort of craft.

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:43.590
And once you've done it once,

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.000
the second one is distinctly easier.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.110
That's the good news.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:50.300
There are people more prolific than I am.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.020
And indeed, one of my heroes,

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.690
the Scottish novelist, John Buchan,

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.950
regarded it as a kind of wasted year

00:08:57.950 --> 00:08:59.760
if he hadn't written a book.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.760
Whereas I think my batting average

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.460
is a book every two
and a half years or so.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:08.120
I'm not exceptionally prolific.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.550
But I did used to look around

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:11.780
at certain universities where I've worked

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.280
and marveled at how few books

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.660
some of my colleagues were able to write.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.030
I don't know what they were
doing the rest of the time.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:19.730
I live to write,

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.990
and reading and writing are the two things

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:24.510
that I do with the greatest facility.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.590
The thinking in the middle
is the difficult bit.

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:29.470
You do the reading and then
the hard bit is the thinking.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:30.630
Once you've figured it out,

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:33.990
the writing is a relatively
straightforward part of the job.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.440
- All right, so between
the personal story,

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:37.273
the writing,

00:09:37.273 --> 00:09:38.970
and a little bit of the thinking,

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:40.210
let's talk about freedom.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.930
So first, I just thought,

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:45.120
telling a bit about your own
personal immigration story

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:46.040
would be kind of interesting.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:47.960
You wrote a blog about it a while back,

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.310
and I think that might be a nice way

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:51.010
to set this whole conversation up.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.570
- Well I've been working
in the United States

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.683
since around 2002.

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.920
I was transatlantic for a while,

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.730
going back and forward very regularly.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:07.100
And it took a while
before I realized that,

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.500
when I was landing at Boston Logan,

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.940
that felt more like coming home,

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:13.040
than landing at Heathrow.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.300
It was kind of a gradual process.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.380
I decided to come to the United States

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:22.060
partly out of a sense
that action was here.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:24.890
In my field, I was writing
a lot of financial history,

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.130
I'd written the history
of the Rothschilds.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:29.520
The English, by and large,
like their historians

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.830
to do kings and queens,
prime ministers, not bankers.

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:38.370
New York kinda got what I was
doing more than London did.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.380
And I was kinda toying with the idea

00:10:41.234 --> 00:10:45.640
of a move to New York
University, when 9/11 happened.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.680
Now the Scots have a propensity

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.970
to march towards the sound of gunfire,

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.970
and I was so pissed at the terrorists

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:55.260
for attacking New York,

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.540
and so determined that they shouldn't,

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.970
in any way, derail my plan,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:02.020
that, in fact. almost immediately

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:05.427
after the 9/11 attacks, I said to NYU,

00:11:05.427 --> 00:11:08.557
"Look, I'm open to an offer
if you're interested."

00:11:08.557 --> 00:11:10.920
And they more or less faxed
one back the next day.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.020
So I resigned from Oxford

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:15.070
and moved to NYU shortly after 9/11

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:18.360
and spent two happy years in New York.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:19.670
Everybody should live in New York

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:21.473
for at least a couple of years.

00:11:22.465 --> 00:11:23.980
And then moved to Harvard.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:28.380
So that was sort of the
beginning of an American journey

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:31.500
that was intellectually captivating.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.270
We went, as you'll recall, very quickly

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:38.490
from the terrorists
attacks to the Iraq war

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:40.540
and debates about American empire,

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:44.170
which dominated the
administration of George W. Bush.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:46.360
Now I'd just written a book
about the British empire,

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.490
so it was natural to start thinking about

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:51.980
those contemporary American issues.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.640
But as I said in that
time, I was transatlantic,

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.200
I was very much the British
historian in the United States,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.070
sort of a stock figure.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.920
It was only gradually
that I began to realize

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:06.750
that my future was in the United States,

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:11.470
and that if I was going to
live here and work here,

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:14.310
and not be a transatlantic
bird of passage,

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:17.970
than I should really go from
being a Green Card holder,

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:19.380
to becoming a citizen.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:23.600
And I did that almost
exactly two years ago.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.050
I just wanna stress,
I'm a legal immigrant.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:28.240
So I jumped through the various hoops

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:31.090
you need to jump through,
held a bunch of visas.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:33.850
And then I became a citizen.

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:37.310
And it seems to many of
my European contemporaries

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:38.690
like a crazy thing to do.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:41.970
What, becoming an American,
under the Trump presidency?

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:42.803
Are you crazy?

00:12:42.803 --> 00:12:46.070
In this time of disruption?

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.970
And my response was, well yeah.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:51.210
Part of the reason for doing that

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:54.500
is the recognition that
you're not signing up

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:56.930
for a particular president,

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.160
you're signing up for the Constitution,

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:02.380
you're signing up for the
ideals of the founders.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.940
And as I've said often to Ayaan,

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.640
I think we immigrants have a better

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:08.990
appreciation of American freedoms

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:11.940
of the first principles of the republic.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:13.600
Better than native born Americans

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.720
'cause we've seen some
of the alternatives.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:20.053
Now for Ayaan, the alternatives
were a great deal worse.

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.430
If you were a girl growing up in Somalia,

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.510
there aren't really too
many freedoms whatsoever.

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:31.510
I grew up in Scotland, I grew
up in the United Kingdom,

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:34.250
and in many ways, you
could say the United States

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:37.960
is a sort of outgrowth
of the British empire.

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.450
So there's a less obvious contrast.

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:41.790
But there still is a contrast.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.730
And I've been aware of it
ever since I was a kid.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.300
- Can you talk about that contrast?

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:47.520
I'm interested in that,

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:51.850
just either from a Scottish
or a British perspective,

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:54.440
to then come to America and see freedom,

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:56.670
or live freedom in a new way.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.680
Like, what types of things,

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:00.320
at a granular level, did you feel?

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.470
Did you feel freer as a historian,

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.160
to talk about certain things?

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:06.360
Or was it just more
about the American ethos,

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:07.283
or what was it?

00:14:08.181 --> 00:14:10.150
- Those are great questions, Dave.

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:12.840
First, there's a little bit of
romance to the United States

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.300
that you mustn't lose sight of.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.330
If you're growing up in Glasgow,

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:18.973
where it rains all the time,

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.370
the romance of the Western,

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.600
the romance of American music,

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.550
the romance of American literature,

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.810
these are powerful attractive forces.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:33.090
And so in the (audio distortion)
Glasgow in the 1970s,

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:36.500
you've got to picture
a spotty teenage Niall

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:41.903
reading Jack Kerouac,
watching Clint Eastwood,

00:14:43.926 --> 00:14:46.750
and listening to a lot of American music.

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:51.460
I became a Jazz enthusiast as a teenager.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:54.110
You can see my double bass
in the background there.

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:56.470
And that's American music, par excellence.

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:58.390
So I think there's a
romance piece to this,

00:14:58.390 --> 00:14:59.870
that one shouldn't underestimate.

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:01.960
But there's an intellectual piece too.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.760
I was steeped in the values

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:07.940
of the Scottish Enlightenment as a boy,

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:09.490
the ideals of Adam Smith,

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:13.210
the very individualistic
culture of 18th century

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:14.710
Glasgow and Edinburgh.

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:17.600
But those values had really declined

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.700
in their significance in Scotland.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:24.370
Socialism had caught fire in Scotland

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.000
in the early 20th century,

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:29.120
I had a great uncle who was a communist,

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:32.770
many other people around us
were die-hard labor supporters.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:34.530
And those Enlightened ideals

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.270
of individual responsibility,
of the free market,

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:39.060
they had already faded,

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.680
to the point that the Scots
hated Margaret Thatcher

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:43.950
for espousing those principles.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.670
Now what's attractive
to an immigrant like me

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:48.970
about the United States is that,

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:51.450
in some places, not
everywhere, but in some places,

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:56.130
you can still find those
Enlightenment values.

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.800
And they're being lived,
they're practiced.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.540
That's a very attractive feature.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:02.490
I think it's also true that

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:04.150
when a Scotsman comes to England,

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:07.130
he's constantly conscious
of his being Scottish.

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:09.080
The English are obsessed with class,

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:10.930
the way Americans are obsessed with race.

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.110
Everything is about class in England.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:14.720
And the question is always, well,

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.810
which school did you go to, really?

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:19.090
And can you just do something

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:21.630
about that funny accent of yours?

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.850
So I never really felt
wholly at home in England,

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:26.670
though I lived for many years
there in Oxford and Cambridge.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.330
And felt much more at home

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:31.570
as soon as I arrived in the United States.

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:34.000
The English believe in
effortless superiority.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.660
They think you should just
sort of be born superior.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:39.330
Americans believe in work,

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:42.150
and they don't begrudge success.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:45.380
And so if you were a kind of ambitious,

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.300
hardworking Scotsman,

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:50.850
this is a more natural
habitat for you than England.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.770
So, for all those reasons,

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:56.250
it didn't take long to feel
at home in the United States,

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.270
perhaps more at home than
I'd ever felt in England.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:01.320
There's quite a lot of Scottish,

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:05.130
in the kind of the
foundations of this country.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:08.220
And when Alexander Hamilton
suddenly became a household name

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.300
thanks to Lin-Manuel Miranda's musical,

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:14.160
that was nice because
he'd been a kind of hero,

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.590
long before he became
famous again, in our family.

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:19.240
My mother's name is Hamilton.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.820
The Scots love to say they
invented the modern world,

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:24.930
and so naturally, that includes
inventing the United States,

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:26.990
or at least its fiscal systems.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:28.770
So for a whole bunch of reasons,

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:32.120
this country was always
powerfully attractive to me.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:36.880
I hitchhiked across America
before I went off to university,

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:38.710
at the age of 17.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.380
I actually reenacted Jack
Kerouac's "On the Road"

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:44.580
with considerable
fidelity to the original.

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:49.030
So yeah, the American dreamers
come in many forms for me.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.740
And that's, I think, ultimately why

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.360
becoming a citizen was exciting.

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:56.040
The whole process was exciting.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:59.053
Even although it had
its surreal side to it.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.230
- Did you notice the
difference on the academic side

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:05.100
when you were with intellectuals,

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:07.210
the way European intellectuals

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:09.190
would perhaps talk about something,

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:11.290
versus the way Americans did?

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:13.860
Maybe a different focus
or anything like that?

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:15.470
- Well you know, I think
there's a disappointment,

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:17.900
which I need to admit to.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:21.140
I came to the United States

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.590
believing that American universities

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:28.330
were the places where
the biggest intellects

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:31.630
had the biggest arguments.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:35.320
And that was part of
the appeal of Harvard.

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:38.580
I admit there was this sense
that Oxford and Cambridge

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:42.970
had been the great universities
of the 19th and 20th century

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:44.920
but now in the 21st century,

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:45.917
you really wanted to be at Harvard.

00:18:45.917 --> 00:18:50.180
And I think over time,
it was a slow process.

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:53.530
I became disillusioned
with the American academy.

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:56.470
And I found that it was becoming

00:18:56.470 --> 00:18:59.860
less and less hospitable to the kind of

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:05.460
rambunctious free inquiry
that motivates me.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:06.780
And now I've reached the point

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.380
where I look back nostalgically

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.740
on the Oxford of the 1980s,

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:15.130
where there was simply no question

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:18.070
of your free speech being circumscribed.

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:19.770
On the contrary in the 1980s,

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:22.280
we kind of found ourselves constantly

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:25.500
pushing the boundaries
of what could be said.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:28.330
As undergraduates, we found our professors

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:32.250
were intellectually an
extraordinarily diverse bunch.

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:34.950
There were the die-hard Marxists,

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:37.420
the kind of Eric Hobsbawm types.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:41.290
And then there were the High
Tories, the Jeremy Cattos.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:44.840
And it was a tremendously
enriching experience.

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:46.700
Academic life in the United States,

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:49.810
especially in the last,
I'll say, five years,

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:52.110
as Jonathan Haidt and others have noted,

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:56.860
has really really been
damaged by a culture

00:19:56.860 --> 00:19:59.660
of what used to be called
political correctness

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:02.640
but I don't think that quite captures it.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.820
A fundamental intolerance
of a deep illiberalism,

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:10.680
a deep hostility to free
debate and free inquiry,

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:14.610
that is killing the great
institutions of American academia.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:18.210
So yeah, I mean, I'm as proud as could be,

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:21.290
of being an American,
but I'm at the same time,

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:23.890
depressed by some of the trends

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:25.250
that I see in the country today,

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.410
because they seem so at odds

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:31.740
with the first principles
of American life.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:33.680
- Is it weird for you to see this spread

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:35.950
across the world at the same time?

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:38.350
Because last year when I was at Oxford,

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:39.900
and I got to speak at Oxford Union,

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:41.930
which I still, it sort
of feels like a dream

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:44.270
that I even got to speak there,

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:45.880
but just being there and being around

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.320
the hundreds of years of history,

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:49.300
and walking around,

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:53.340
and you feel this thing that you guys have

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:54.990
that we have much less of, obviously,

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:56.190
because of the breadth of history,

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:59.970
you feel this generation after generation

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:02.910
of caring about knowledge and
history and all these things.

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:04.890
And even just in the last couple of days,

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:06.860
I've seen these articles that Oxford,

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:10.560
their professors are pushing
to have more lenient grades

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:12.350
for students of color,

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.640
and just the rest of the sort of,

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:16.850
what I would call, intellectual rot,

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:20.120
that we've seen across these schools.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.210
It must be particularly painful for you

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.223
to see it happen at a place like Oxford.

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:30.010
- I think so, although I
sometimes get the sense

00:21:31.198 --> 00:21:36.050
that it's not that
deep, the rot in Oxford.

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:39.660
For example, I was very pleased
that the Vice Chancellor,

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:42.700
Louise Richardson, and other
leaders of the university,

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:45.630
dismissed again the calls to tear down

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:50.630
a statue of Cecil Rhodes
that stands in Oriel College.

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:53.730
Cambridge, I think, has
been rather more inclined

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:58.730
to appease these forces of iconoclasm.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:02.800
It is iconoclasm, the
tearing down of statues.

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:06.810
And I know that I'm a conservative,

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:09.510
when I see statues being torn down,

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:11.520
and a little part of me winces,

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.360
it doesn't really much
matter who the statue is.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.603
I prefer statues to be built.

00:22:17.919 --> 00:22:21.200
And I think Oxford has held
the line there, thus far.

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:22.620
But as is always the case,

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:27.310
there are elements within
any academic society that,

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:31.810
let's see the opportunities
in the ideologies of wokeness.

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:36.010
See ways in which they can
advance their own careers

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:40.440
by championing these
outlandish and illiberal ideas.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:45.440
The great danger is
that it becomes a norm,

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:49.970
that a university should be
intellectually homogenous.

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:53.010
That we should pursue
every kind of diversity,

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:57.210
except intellectual and
ideological diversity.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:59.530
And I worry that eventually,

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:02.020
what has been happening
in American universities,

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:06.980
will overcome the healthier
forces at Oxford and Cambridge.

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:09.700
That's not to say that we couldn't

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:12.570
do more at these universities,

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:16.210
to make them attractive
to, and appealing to,

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:17.720
people from minority communities,

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:19.770
and from the white working class,

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:22.820
which is notably
underrepresented at these places.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.180
But the mistake that's
always made in these debates

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:28.280
is to think that the problem
lies with the universities,

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:30.960
rather than with the systems of education

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:33.190
that simply discourage students,

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:36.110
or make them unable to
apply to elite institutions.

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:38.490
I've always said I believe passionately

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:40.800
in educational opportunity,

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.960
and the social mobility that it creates.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.860
My parents were the
first in their families

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:46.550
to go to university.

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:50.730
And what I see in the
United States and in the UK,

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:55.530
are failing schools that don't
allow talented young people,

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:57.170
whether they're from minority communities

00:23:57.170 --> 00:23:58.100
or from the working class,

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:01.510
to get anywhere close to
applying to elite universities.

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:03.170
That's what we need to fix.

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:04.450
And there are lots of ways of doing it,

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:07.080
but one doesn't hear those discussed.

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:09.530
- So I know we could spend eight hours

00:24:09.530 --> 00:24:12.720
dissecting wokeness and the rest of it.

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:14.110
But I wanna shift from it a little bit,

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.330
but I just have one other
question on the woke thing,

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:19.070
since we've treaded into those waters.

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:23.350
As a conservative academic,
you were obviously,

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:25.370
and we talked a little
bit about this last time,

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:29.330
on the outside of what
most academic circles

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:33.983
think is acceptable, even
in history department.

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:40.000
Do you think that conservatives
have a better defense

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:42.680
against wokeness than liberals?

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:46.050
So for example, Jon Haidt,
who I've had on the show,

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:47.720
and many of the other academics

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.860
that you know that I've talked to,

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.410
that come from the more Liberal side,

00:24:51.410 --> 00:24:54.770
I sense that although they're
making good arguments,

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:57.530
I don't think they have
good defenses against this,

00:24:57.530 --> 00:25:00.590
because of the sort of
openness of liberalism,

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:02.600
that it's being used against itself.

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:05.170
And I do sense that conservatism,

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:09.150
as a world view, or as
a philosophical lens,

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:13.050
does have some protections
that liberalism doesn't.

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:14.373
Do you agree with that?

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:19.470
- Well it's true that we wouldn't
be in quite this situation

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:22.780
if it hadn't been for liberal professors,

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:25.640
giving jobs to progressive professors,

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:30.130
who then gave jobs to Marxists
and cultural Marxists.

00:25:30.130 --> 00:25:32.360
I mean, conservative academic,
conservative professors,

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:33.830
these are becoming oxymorons,

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:37.640
because there are so
few conservatives left

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:39.580
in the American academy.

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:41.120
I mean the figures are clear,

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:44.480
it's not like this is an old
problem that we've always had.

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:48.790
Over time, the ratio of registered
Democrats to Republicans,

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:50.660
has risen steadily to the point that

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:52.900
you can't calculate it in some departments

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:55.730
'cause there are zero Republicans.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:26:00.350
And I think that trend
broadly is explicable

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:03.680
because liberals felt
that they should hire

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:06.640
people to the left of them,

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:09.630
underestimating the extent
to which those people

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:14.630
would fundamentally differ
on the principles of,

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:19.230
say, the project of
historical scholarship.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:21.630
It is not the goal of a history department

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:25.320
to impose on the past,

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:28.870
the ideals of the progressives of 2020,

00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:31.920
that's the ultimate
condescension to the past.

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:36.160
Conservatives have understood
better, I think, the dangers.

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:38.990
And the tragedy has
been that conservatives

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:40.820
have been poorly organized.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:44.390
I mean the extinction of conservatives in,

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:45.980
say, history departments,

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:48.560
isn't just the fault of
liberals hiring progressives.

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:51.320
It's also that the
conservatives did poorly

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.980
when it came to the
most basic organization

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:55.940
that has to happen in academia.

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:57.940
The patronage networks,

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.840
getting your students into good positions.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:03.690
I don't think conservatives
were very good at that.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:06.890
My conservative mentors were
wonderful intellectuals.

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:08.700
People like Norman Stone.

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:10.860
But Norman would not
be remembered, I think,

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:13.680
for his organizational skills.

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:16.110
For his writing, certainly.

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:19.330
But definitely not for his
deftness at academic politics.

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:21.130
So it's partly I think
because conservatives

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:23.130
were rather too busy writing books

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:25.570
and perhaps occasionally
drinking bottles of wine,

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:29.020
and not working hard enough
at the committee stages

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:31.473
to make sure that the
right candidates got hired.

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:35.620
- It's interesting and I think,

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:37.750
that right there almost
explains everything

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:39.890
happening in the world right now.

00:27:39.890 --> 00:27:43.060
That we let all these
kids out after watching

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:46.080
academia sort of crumble, and here we are.

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.930
But shifting from that,

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:51.170
from a historian's perspective,

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:55.310
what do you think that the
average American should know

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:57.520
about the history of America,

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:00.230
that we don't know, or
that we under-appreciate?

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:01.630
Because you know, we
seem to live in a time

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:03.990
where we're erasing our own history,

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:05.490
and we're taking down monuments

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:09.360
and we're destroying the names
of people that were flawed

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.360
but that were just people of their time?

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:15.560
- Well I think the challenge here

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:20.560
is that history is not monochrome.

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:25.970
The history of any great society or state

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:28.850
can't be told as a eulogy,

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.690
nor as a kind of criminal indictment.

00:28:32.690 --> 00:28:36.180
And the good historian
of the United States,

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:39.370
or for that matter, of the United Kingdom,

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:43.930
acknowledges that there are
debits as well as credits,

00:28:43.930 --> 00:28:47.730
that there's shadows, there's
dark sides to the story,

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:49.540
as well as bright sides.

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:54.440
And this is a challenge that
I didn't think was difficult

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.720
when I was writing earlier in my career.

00:28:56.720 --> 00:28:58.550
I wrote a history of the British empire,

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:01.740
which included a chapter
on the American Revolution.

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:05.460
And the book sets out very plainly,

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:07.400
that there are many stains on the history

00:29:07.400 --> 00:29:10.530
of the British empire,
including, of course,

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:15.530
the central role of slavery
in the southern colonies

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:19.630
that became the southern
states of the United States.

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.140
But the point of that book,

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:22.527
and I make the same argument about

00:29:22.527 --> 00:29:24.380
the history of the United States,

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:28.260
is that the benefits outweigh the costs.

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:32.220
That the successes were
more important, ultimately,

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:33.920
than the failures.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:36.090
The project that the New York Times

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:38.830
was given a Pulitzer for this year,

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:41.770
of recasting the history
of the United States,

00:29:41.770 --> 00:29:44.880
in terms of slavery as
the primary narrative,

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:46.460
the 1619 Project,

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:50.190
seems to me just to be
fundamentally historically wrong.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:52.210
It's a distortion.

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:55.250
This isn't to say that
slavery didn't matter,

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:57.860
it just doesn't mean it's not important.

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:00.720
It's just that if you
were to try to explain

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:05.440
the significance of the United
States to a visiting Martian,

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:07.470
you wouldn't start with slavery,

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:11.370
because that wasn't really
an especially unique feature

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:12.710
of the United States.

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:16.060
There was slavery and it
lasted much longer in Brazil,

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:17.960
there was slavery and it was far harsher

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:20.400
in the Caribbean colonies

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:22.650
of Britain and other European states.

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:24.500
So what's the defining characteristic,

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:27.930
what's the thing that makes
the United States distinctive?

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:30.400
It's not slavery,

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:34.113
anymore than if you looked at
Western civilization broadly.

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:37.750
It would be true to say that imperialism

00:30:37.750 --> 00:30:39.120
is the defining characteristic.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:40.500
Everybody did empire,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:44.370
just as slavery goes way back before 1619,

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:45.590
all the way to ancient times.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:47.950
And by the way, still exists.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:49.490
There is still slavery.

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:53.270
There are slave markets
where people are sold.

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:55.120
There are slave economies,

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:57.240
which rely on slave labor today.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:59.180
So it can't really be true to say

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:00.240
that the interesting thing,

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:01.410
the thing you really need to know

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:03.720
about the United States is slavery.

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:06.000
But the interesting thing
about the United States

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:10.170
is that it represented an
experiment in governance,

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:14.370
a radical experiment based on
ideas from the Enlightenment,

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:17.540
and earlier ideas from the
Protestant Reformation,

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:18.970
it's fair to say.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:22.580
And this produced an
extraordinary Constitution,

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:26.510
which has stood up to all
the stresses and the strains

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:32.100
of history since the late 18th century.

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:35.070
And understanding that
peculiar Constitution,

00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:36.630
that very distinctive document,

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.330
with its separation of powers,

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:40.140
and its careful preservations

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:41.170
of individual (audio distortion),

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:44.050
that should be your top priority

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:46.100
if you're studying American history.

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:48.690
Not to the exclusion of the bad stuff.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:52.430
There's plenty of bad stuff
that we need to study.

00:31:52.430 --> 00:31:55.710
But I don't think we should
be teaching young Americans,

00:31:55.710 --> 00:31:56.627
from how it Howard Zinn's

00:31:56.627 --> 00:31:58.590
"A People's History of the United States,"

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:01.090
in which the negatives are constantly

00:32:01.090 --> 00:32:02.540
emphasized over the positives.

00:32:02.540 --> 00:32:04.300
That just seems to me to miss the point

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:06.540
of the American story completely.

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:09.280
- Are you worried that the
extraordinary pressures

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:10.980
that we're facing right now,

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:14.960
between the pandemic,
between riots and protests,

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:19.960
between polarization that
really is off the charts,

00:32:20.110 --> 00:32:21.560
and then throw in social media,

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:23.190
which is just gas on the flame,

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:25.210
are you worried that those pressures

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:27.410
could snap that project in a way

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:30.310
that it hasn't been snapped before?

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:33.120
Or certainly in the last
hundred years, let's say?

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:34.460
- Dave, let me give you a somewhat

00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:35.980
surprising answer to that question.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:38.160
I'd be worried if we weren't worried.

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:39.960
One of the things about this country

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.420
that's very distinctive is that

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:44.730
we're always worried that
the Republic's gonna end.

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:49.370
We're always kinda fretting
about the coming tyranny,

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:51.133
the impending civil war.

00:32:52.121 --> 00:32:53.980
And thus far, there
has been one civil war,

00:32:53.980 --> 00:32:55.763
and no dictatorships.

00:32:56.720 --> 00:32:58.300
And that's kind of encouraging.

00:32:58.300 --> 00:33:01.280
I think that's partly
because we worry about it.

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:04.210
And I think it's a healthy
feature of American life also,

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:06.150
to expect decline.

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:09.400
People have been predicting the
decline of the United States

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.100
throughout my life.

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:13.770
It was a preoccupation of Henry Kissinger,

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:16.880
whose biography, I'm in
the midst of writing.

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.280
In the 1970s, it seems
as if the United States

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:22.950
was being torn apart by Vietnam,

00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:25.900
torn apart by its racial divisions,

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:27.770
torn apart by a president

00:33:27.770 --> 00:33:30.410
who had violated his oath office,

00:33:30.410 --> 00:33:32.620
all of that we've seen before.

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:36.600
And I think it's part of the
way the United States works,

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:38.330
that we're always expecting

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:40.220
the disintegration of the Republic

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:41.680
and the descend into civil war.

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:44.610
And I think we'll have
to start being nervous

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:46.840
if we stop worrying about that stuff.

00:33:46.840 --> 00:33:48.570
But as long as we have-

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:50.520
My old friend, Andrew Sullivan,

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:53.540
so fretful about the coming Trump tyranny,

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:56.590
that he doesn't quite spot
the tyranny of the left,

00:33:56.590 --> 00:33:58.660
until it's right up behind him.

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:01.290
I mean that's kind of the
predicament that I think

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:05.130
a lot of liberals and
conservatives find themselves in.

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:08.280
That they were so indignant and dismayed

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:10.700
by Trump back in 2016,

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:13.580
that they slightly underestimated

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:16.970
the rather more operationally worrying

00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:21.040
tyrannical tendencies of the radical left.

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:23.780
So yeah, I think we should
keep worrying about this stuff.

00:34:23.780 --> 00:34:25.360
And as long as we're worrying about it,

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:26.803
we'll probably be okay.

00:34:27.700 --> 00:34:31.130
- Yeah, does it also strike you as odd,

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:34.110
or maybe obvious,
relative to social media,

00:34:34.110 --> 00:34:36.880
that when something happens here,

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:38.870
that it suddenly happens everywhere?

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:41.480
I mean, we've all seen the videos now

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:44.370
of literally police officers in London,

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:46.190
being hunted down by mobs.

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:49.700
That has nothing to do,
in any concrete sense,

00:34:49.700 --> 00:34:51.450
with what happened to George Floyd,

00:34:51.450 --> 00:34:54.170
and yet, it seems like if
there's an incident here,

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:55.823
it's now exported everywhere.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:35:00.850
- I think future historians
will wonder why it was,

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:02.860
that in the midst of a pandemic,

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:05.290
which is far from over,

00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:09.760
Americans decided to have a
debate about police violence

00:35:10.690 --> 00:35:13.410
towards African Americans.

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:15.260
Not to say that that's not

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:17.560
an important issue to think about,

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:20.140
but it seems an odd time
to have that debate,

00:35:20.140 --> 00:35:23.510
and to start debates about
defunding the police,

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:25.320
when we should really be wondering

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:27.040
what went wrong with
the Department of Health

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:29.260
and Human Services and CDC

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:32.120
when they ought to have taken far better

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:35.720
and earlier action to
contain the contagion.

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:36.880
There's something strange,

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:38.450
it's a non sequitur,

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:41.650
that in the midst of this
massive public health crisis,

00:35:41.650 --> 00:35:44.320
which has killed nearly 120,000 Americans

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.570
and will probably kill another 120,000

00:35:46.570 --> 00:35:47.600
before the end of the year,

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:49.770
we're having this debate.

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:52.130
But the second part I'd make is that,

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:57.060
the internet has created
a new kind of contagion.

00:35:57.060 --> 00:35:59.160
As I said earlier, quoting my son,

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:01.030
there are two pandemics.

00:36:01.030 --> 00:36:03.760
And the second pandemic, in fact,

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:07.120
predates the murder of George Floyd,

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:10.240
because it's a pandemic of protests,

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:13.720
that you could actually see in Hong Kong,

00:36:13.720 --> 00:36:18.720
in San Diego, in Barcelona, in
cities like Beirut last year.

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:21.750
And what is interesting to me is that

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:25.070
the mode of protest is
consistently the same.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:27.750
The content varies from place to place,

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:31.560
but this quality of protest to go viral,

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:33.873
styles of protests to be copied,

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:38.670
from Hong Kong to Minneapolis,
is really interesting.

00:36:38.670 --> 00:36:40.360
I mean, historically, the police

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:42.820
had better communications
than demonstrators,

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:46.820
for most of the second
half of the 20th century.

00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:48.280
That the police had walkie talkies,

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:51.010
and the demonstrators were
lucky if they had megaphones.

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:53.490
But the smartphone has changed that.

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:56.640
And it's allowed protestors
to really organize

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:58.080
these acephalous networks,

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:00.140
where there is no obvious leader.

00:37:00.140 --> 00:37:03.160
And actually have superior
comms to the police.

00:37:03.160 --> 00:37:05.000
So I think what's interesting about

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:08.240
the great eruption of
protests that we've seen

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:11.360
in the last month in the United States,

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:15.080
it is that, actually,
it's, in terms of its form,

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:18.810
quite a close copy of the Hong
Kong protests of last year.

00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:21.500
It's just that the content's different.

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:25.160
And I think you can keep these
sorts of protest waves going

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:27.760
as long as people have viral video,

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:30.820
as well as sort of tips
on organizing a protest

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:32.850
that they can simply download

00:37:32.850 --> 00:37:34.990
and apply it to their context.

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:37.110
That I think is the really
interesting feature.

00:37:37.110 --> 00:37:41.800
It's not the content of the
protests that's the same,

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:44.180
as in previous waves of revolution.

00:37:44.180 --> 00:37:47.070
Here, it's just the form
of protest that's the same.

00:37:47.070 --> 00:37:49.420
The content varies from place to place.

00:37:49.420 --> 00:37:50.253
- Yeah, it's interesting.

00:37:50.253 --> 00:37:53.220
So you're saying you're not
worried because we worry.

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:54.570
And then on the other hand,

00:37:54.570 --> 00:37:57.370
there is this other pathogen or virus,

00:37:57.370 --> 00:37:58.210
or whatever you wanna call it,

00:37:58.210 --> 00:38:00.580
that really does make this something

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:03.333
that we've just never dealt with before.

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:08.220
- I think we've dealt with the
revolutionary crowd before.

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:10.070
It's just the revolutionary crowd

00:38:10.070 --> 00:38:11.980
didn't have smartphones before.

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:14.020
I was fascinated by the way

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:15.640
in which the Hong Kong protestors

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:18.610
outsmarted the police there, last year.

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:21.470
Partly by constantly changing
the form of the protest,

00:38:21.470 --> 00:38:24.750
partly by not having leaders
that you could just round up,

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:27.020
partly by shifting away from violence,

00:38:27.020 --> 00:38:29.030
when violence was alienating local people.

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:31.770
And I think we saw
something of that happen.

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:34.520
Because in June, there
was a period earlier on

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:37.180
when the protests were quite
violent, and there was looting,

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:38.430
and that actually was quite

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:40.900
unhelpful to the protestors' cause.

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:42.230
And then you saw a shift

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:45.420
in the style of protests in many places.

00:38:45.420 --> 00:38:47.160
So this shape-shifting quality

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:50.400
of the crowd with smartphones,

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:52.630
is an interesting thing to observe.

00:38:52.630 --> 00:38:56.640
I don't think it's necessarily
a disastrous phenomenon.

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:58.170
Because in some context,

00:38:58.170 --> 00:39:03.170
these protests achieve
successful outcomes.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:06.173
You know, crowds aren't always bad.

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:10.420
There are even occasions
when you can justify

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:11.670
pulling down a statue,

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:15.720
think New York, 1776.

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:19.360
But my sense is that on the specific issue

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:22.320
that is being addressed in
the United States today,

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:25.710
which has to do with changing
the way the police operate,

00:39:25.710 --> 00:39:28.420
there is a big disconnect between

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:30.740
what is said by protestors,

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:33.890
and indeed by Black Lives Matter,

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:36.620
and what we know from good social science,

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:38.790
the real problem is.

00:39:38.790 --> 00:39:41.730
And I do think it's difficult for a crowd

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:43.230
to read social science

00:39:43.230 --> 00:39:47.420
and arrive at actionable
policy recommendations.

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:48.963
That tends not to happen.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:53.010
- Is that the biggest danger
that we have going right now?

00:39:53.010 --> 00:39:55.060
That there's a certain set of people

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.520
that it doesn't matter what
the social science says,

00:39:57.520 --> 00:39:59.580
it doesn't matter what the facts say.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:03.910
That their ideology overrides all of that.

00:40:03.910 --> 00:40:05.810
So this sort of goes
back to what I was saying

00:40:05.810 --> 00:40:08.180
about our liberal academics friends.

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:10.850
It's like, you guys can make
sound arguments all day long,

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:12.550
that's great, I love it,

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:14.290
I'll do it all day with you.

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:18.110
But that doesn't actually
address the problem

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:20.210
because there's something
else going on here,

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:22.773
that can't be rationalized out of.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.330
- Right, and I think
the case of Roland Fryer

00:40:27.330 --> 00:40:29.660
illustrates many of the predicaments

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:31.230
that we find ourselves in today.

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.150
Roland, who wrote some pioneering papers

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:38.470
as a Harvard economist, in 2016,

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:41.820
published an extraordinarily
important study showing that,

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:45.280
while the police did disproportionately

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:50.280
shove, push, and otherwise
manhandle African Americans,

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:52.420
compared with white suspects,

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:57.420
they did not disproportionately
shoot African Americans.

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:02.540
And that finding was an
extremely important one,

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:04.920
since at the core of Black Lives Matter,

00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:07.480
was a claim that the police
disproportionately use

00:41:07.480 --> 00:41:09.020
lethal violence against black people.

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:10.800
And it turns out not to be true.

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:13.720
But what became of Roland
after that publication?

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.730
He knew there would be
blowback, and there was.

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:18.930
But what actually happened was not at all

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:21.700
what he or I or his friends had expected.

00:41:21.700 --> 00:41:25.890
What happened was that he was
accused of sexual harassments

00:41:26.780 --> 00:41:28.400
by a former employee,

00:41:28.400 --> 00:41:30.040
white, incidentally,

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:31.930
and then found himself sucked into

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:35.870
a Title IX investigation at Harvard,

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:39.550
which I think was actually a
grave miscarriage of justice.

00:41:39.550 --> 00:41:42.700
And it's hard to avoid the suspicion

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:45.000
that if Roland's research had arrived

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:47.340
at a more politically correct conclusions,

00:41:47.340 --> 00:41:50.200
that were more compatible
with progressive narratives,

00:41:50.200 --> 00:41:52.270
perhaps that might not have happened.

00:41:52.270 --> 00:41:55.050
I certainly think that there
is something to be asked,

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:56.800
some hard questions to be asked,

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:59.500
about why an African American professor,

00:41:59.500 --> 00:42:03.083
who started life in the wrong
side of the tracks in Texas,

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:08.070
has had first hand experiences
of police brutality,

00:42:08.070 --> 00:42:11.760
why he should have ended up
being treated in this way

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:15.010
by mostly white progressive administrators

00:42:15.010 --> 00:42:20.010
at the country's most well-known
and eminent university.

00:42:20.500 --> 00:42:23.900
It's something that's been
on my mind for some time now

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:26.920
since the case against him was brought.

00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:28.770
And I sense that ultimately,

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:31.560
we won't really be able to arrive

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:34.030
at a serious discussion of these issues,

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:38.000
data-based, and also based
on personal experience,

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:42.343
as long as Roland's being
canceled in the name of Me Too.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:45.870
- So in a weird way, do
you need the institutions

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:47.860
to crumble so that
something better can happen?

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:49.380
Like it seems like we're sort of just

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:51.810
in a slow crumble phase right now.

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:54.670
There doesn't seem to be
any great institution,

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:57.230
whether it's Harvard
or the New York Times,

00:42:57.230 --> 00:42:59.260
or a series of other things,

00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:00.720
that we sort of all look to

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:04.020
as the guardians of the Republic.

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:05.300
They're all sort of crumbling.

00:43:05.300 --> 00:43:08.280
Do you think they have
to crumble, to re-set?

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:10.840
Or do you think they can actually

00:43:13.760 --> 00:43:14.870
just re-put together?

00:43:14.870 --> 00:43:16.970
Just put together the pieces again?

00:43:16.970 --> 00:43:18.520
- Well I think one of the lessons

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:20.840
of American history is that, at its best,

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:24.610
the United States
creates new institutions,

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:29.610
rather than just gives
the old ones makeovers.

00:43:29.740 --> 00:43:32.780
I think one of the sad
features of our time

00:43:32.780 --> 00:43:37.110
is that the great Plutocrats
of our gilded age,

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:39.540
have not created new universities,

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:43.320
as their predecessors did
in the late 19th century.

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:46.600
But have simply given money
to the existing institutions,

00:43:46.600 --> 00:43:48.190
and in particular to institutions that

00:43:48.190 --> 00:43:50.120
already had tons of money.

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:55.120
And my recommendation to
today's robber barons,

00:43:55.570 --> 00:43:57.350
or if you wanna put it politely,

00:43:57.350 --> 00:44:00.510
titans of Silicon Valley
and Wall Street is,

00:44:00.510 --> 00:44:04.420
just remember the
examples of the Carnegies,

00:44:04.420 --> 00:44:06.890
and do some innovation here,

00:44:06.890 --> 00:44:08.310
build some new institutions.

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:09.520
You don't like what's happening

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:11.120
at Harvard or Yale or Stanford,

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:12.820
well, there's nothing to stop you

00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:14.700
creating a new institution.

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:17.470
Because that's the American way.

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:18.850
What Tocqueville noticed,

00:44:18.850 --> 00:44:21.650
the great French political theorist,

00:44:21.650 --> 00:44:22.620
Alexis de Tocqueville,

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:24.780
when he came to the United States

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:28.840
before writing his great
book, "Democracy in America,"

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:31.300
was that Americans then,

00:44:31.300 --> 00:44:36.300
did not expect the government
to solve their problems.

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:40.660
They did it through associational life.

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:44.430
And for me, the most striking
feature of this American story

00:44:44.430 --> 00:44:47.750
is this readiness for
people at the local level

00:44:47.750 --> 00:44:50.410
to deal with problems locally,

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:54.000
to build civic institutions locally,

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:56.980
and not to expect the federal government

00:44:56.980 --> 00:44:59.470
or for that matter, Harvard
or the New York Times,

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:01.820
to come up with solutions.

00:45:01.820 --> 00:45:06.610
So I desperately feel a
need for new institutions.

00:45:06.610 --> 00:45:09.200
And not only at the level of colleges.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:11.270
I think we need new schools.

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:14.170
There's been some innovation
in that direction,

00:45:14.170 --> 00:45:15.500
in the Charter-School Movement,

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:17.770
but it's still really a drop in the bucket

00:45:17.770 --> 00:45:19.890
of what needs to happen to improve

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:21.410
American secondary education,

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:22.990
which is the real weakness

00:45:22.990 --> 00:45:25.990
that this country is burdened with.

00:45:25.990 --> 00:45:29.120
So I think we just need
to remind ourselves that,

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:31.400
in its original design,

00:45:31.400 --> 00:45:33.360
the United States was supposed to be,

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:35.350
a, decentralized,

00:45:35.350 --> 00:45:37.750
with b, small government,

00:45:37.750 --> 00:45:42.180
c, a vibrant associational
life and religious life,

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:45.900
and crucially, a sort
of constant formation

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:47.493
of new institutions.

00:45:47.493 --> 00:45:50.310
That that was what you did,
if you'd been successful,

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:53.160
if you'd had the American
capitalist dream.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:55.800
Giving back involve more than just

00:45:55.800 --> 00:45:59.010
a new building at Harvard
with your name on it.

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:02.140
Giving back meant a new university,

00:46:02.140 --> 00:46:04.400
something that hadn't existed before.

00:46:04.400 --> 00:46:06.150
That's what we seemed to have lost.

00:46:06.150 --> 00:46:09.430
And I'm really struck by
that lack of new institutions

00:46:09.430 --> 00:46:11.440
as one of the pathologies,
one of the signs,

00:46:11.440 --> 00:46:13.433
of our recent degeneration.

00:46:14.497 --> 00:46:16.650
- So you don't think that
the Harvard endowment,

00:46:16.650 --> 00:46:18.810
which is worth what, $40 billion,

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:21.320
you don't think they
need a couple more bucks?

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:26.320
- Well, far be it for me to
advise America's philanthropists

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:28.460
where they should put their money.

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:32.610
But if they applied the kind
of due diligence standards

00:46:32.610 --> 00:46:34.100
that they apply when they're making

00:46:34.100 --> 00:46:36.200
investments to their philanthropy,

00:46:36.200 --> 00:46:39.600
then I think they might
at least think twice.

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:42.500
'Cause the return on
investment that you get

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:46.220
from donating to an already
established institution,

00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:48.110
just can't be that high.

00:46:48.110 --> 00:46:52.181
I mean, what real marginal impact

00:46:52.181 --> 00:46:54.750
does a million dollars make to Harvard?

00:46:54.750 --> 00:46:57.360
Whereas if you were to use the money

00:46:57.360 --> 00:46:58.900
to create something new,

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:01.440
the return on investment would
potentially be far higher.

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:04.820
So I think there's a need
for new institutions.

00:47:04.820 --> 00:47:06.650
I think it would help revitalize.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:08.640
Just as we should have new statues.

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:10.380
If you don't like the old ones,

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:12.220
why not build some new statues

00:47:12.220 --> 00:47:16.130
rather than wasting your
energies on vandalism?

00:47:16.130 --> 00:47:19.590
One of my favorite
sculptors, Sandy Stoddart,

00:47:19.590 --> 00:47:21.843
who's a great proponent of iconoclasm,

00:47:23.125 --> 00:47:25.270
created two wonderful
statues in Edinburgh,

00:47:25.270 --> 00:47:26.290
the Scottish capital.

00:47:26.290 --> 00:47:29.750
One is of David Hume, the
other is of Adam Smith.

00:47:29.750 --> 00:47:30.860
They're beautiful statues,

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:32.150
they stand on the Royal Mile.

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:33.630
Tourists assume they've been there

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:35.423
for at least a hundred years,

00:47:35.423 --> 00:47:37.840
(audio distortion) barely 10 years old.

00:47:37.840 --> 00:47:39.230
And I think Sandy Stoddart

00:47:39.230 --> 00:47:41.960
could be well employed
in the United States,

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:43.790
creating some beautiful new statues,

00:47:43.790 --> 00:47:45.740
to whatever it is that we think

00:47:45.740 --> 00:47:47.390
should be the heroes of our time.

00:47:47.390 --> 00:47:51.690
That would be a far better
employment of public energy

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:54.190
than the kind of vandalism
that we see these days.

00:47:55.389 --> 00:47:57.770
- So since this is our July 4th week show,

00:47:57.770 --> 00:48:00.570
and I've got a noted historian on,

00:48:00.570 --> 00:48:03.110
give me a little American history

00:48:03.110 --> 00:48:06.230
that maybe the average
person watching this,

00:48:06.230 --> 00:48:08.900
give me something, an anecdote, a story,

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:11.540
a moment that the average
person watching this

00:48:11.540 --> 00:48:13.210
maybe doesn't know that much about.

00:48:13.210 --> 00:48:14.070
That they should know.

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:17.830
Is there something that
sticks out to you as,

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:20.730
oy, more people should know
about that story, that moment?

00:48:22.250 --> 00:48:26.110
- Well I suspect, in a crowded field,

00:48:26.110 --> 00:48:31.110
I'd be bound to choose
one of those episodes

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:37.220
that really paved the
way for the United States

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:41.960
to become, not just a
hugely successful republic,

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:45.050
more successful than
any previous republic,

00:48:45.050 --> 00:48:48.810
but a great power, even a super power.

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:52.800
And the temptation for
republics, historically,

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:55.463
has always been at a certain point,

00:48:56.620 --> 00:48:59.760
for the temptations power

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:02.433
to steer the republic to tyranny.

00:49:03.480 --> 00:49:08.380
This very nearly happened during
Harry Truman's presidency,

00:49:08.380 --> 00:49:10.790
at the time of the Korean war,

00:49:10.790 --> 00:49:14.563
when a rogue figure, Douglas MacArthur,

00:49:16.050 --> 00:49:19.180
quarreled with Truman about the
direction of the Korean war.

00:49:19.180 --> 00:49:20.970
MacArthur actually was
in favor of dropping

00:49:20.970 --> 00:49:24.650
atomic bombs on China to end the war.

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:27.610
And it came to a clash,

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:31.630
that to another republic,
that might have been fatal.

00:49:31.630 --> 00:49:33.860
Think of the fate of the Roman republic,

00:49:33.860 --> 00:49:36.210
which ultimately became an empire,

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:41.210
as the mighty figure
of Augustus ultimately

00:49:42.650 --> 00:49:47.050
sucked the life out of the
institutions of the republic.

00:49:47.050 --> 00:49:49.570
But I think that the
showdown that happened then,

00:49:49.570 --> 00:49:51.710
between Truman and MacArthur,

00:49:51.710 --> 00:49:55.040
Truman's successful
assertion of the primacy

00:49:55.040 --> 00:49:57.840
of the civilian authority,

00:49:57.840 --> 00:50:00.810
is a hugely important
moment in American history.

00:50:00.810 --> 00:50:05.710
Truman was a great president
in a whole range of ways.

00:50:05.710 --> 00:50:07.817
He is best known for the famous line,

00:50:07.817 --> 00:50:10.010
"The buck stops here."

00:50:10.010 --> 00:50:13.440
But it stopped there in
a very important way.

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:17.630
When Truman asserted the
primacy of the presidency

00:50:17.630 --> 00:50:21.770
against the charismatic
military figure who,

00:50:21.770 --> 00:50:24.647
when he returned to the United States,

00:50:24.647 --> 00:50:27.030
had a ticker tape parade in New York.

00:50:27.030 --> 00:50:29.100
You watch the scenes of MacArthur's return

00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:30.420
at that time of crisis,

00:50:30.420 --> 00:50:33.160
when it seemed he might make
a presidential bid himself,

00:50:33.160 --> 00:50:35.240
you realize that the United States

00:50:35.240 --> 00:50:39.820
was teetering on the
brink of a Roman outcome,

00:50:39.820 --> 00:50:41.350
but pulled back.

00:50:41.350 --> 00:50:43.800
And I want to emphasize that episode,

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:45.170
which isn't well-known,

00:50:45.170 --> 00:50:47.450
because you should always keep in mind

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:49.380
the history of what hasn't happened,

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:52.380
what didn't happen, even if it nearly did.

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:54.370
You have to keep it alive in your mind

00:50:54.370 --> 00:50:58.400
all the times that this
republic might have fallen

00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:03.400
from the path of democracy
and the rule of law.

00:51:03.540 --> 00:51:07.340
And remind yourself,
when you're feeling down,

00:51:07.340 --> 00:51:09.830
as some people occasionally do feel down

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:12.260
contemplating our politics,

00:51:12.260 --> 00:51:16.690
that American democracy has
never been a fairy story.

00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:18.930
When Charles Dickens
came to the United States

00:51:18.930 --> 00:51:19.790
in the 19th century,

00:51:19.790 --> 00:51:23.910
he was appalled by the
viciousness of American politics,

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:28.490
by its venality, by the rule
of money, by the corrupt press.

00:51:28.490 --> 00:51:29.840
We shouldn't tell ourselves that

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:32.710
there's some golden age in the past

00:51:32.710 --> 00:51:34.520
that we've somehow lapsed from.

00:51:34.520 --> 00:51:37.790
I think that's a very common
failing that Americans have.

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:39.610
They're always telling themselves

00:51:39.610 --> 00:51:42.560
it was so much better
in those good old days.

00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:44.960
Pick a decade, the 50s, the 80s.

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:46.070
But I don't think that's the right way

00:51:46.070 --> 00:51:48.050
to think about American history.

00:51:48.050 --> 00:51:50.830
I think it's better to realize
that the republic experiment,

00:51:50.830 --> 00:51:53.610
the with this particular Constitution,

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:56.240
was always going to be fraught with peril.

00:51:56.240 --> 00:51:58.200
But the Founding Fathers knew

00:51:58.200 --> 00:51:59.500
that somebody like Donald Trump

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:01.440
would become president at some point.

00:52:01.440 --> 00:52:02.770
That was why they designed

00:52:02.770 --> 00:52:04.140
the Constitution the way they did.

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:06.780
Alexander Hamilton was
very explicit about that

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:08.920
in the Federalist Papers and elsewhere.

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:10.960
So, I think, as an immigrant,

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:12.810
my advice to Americans is,

00:52:12.810 --> 00:52:15.510
don't have this fantasy, America,

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:18.790
that you keep failing to live up to,

00:52:18.790 --> 00:52:20.620
that you feel you've somehow missed.

00:52:20.620 --> 00:52:21.900
'Cause it never existed.

00:52:21.900 --> 00:52:24.090
There's never been that fantasy, America.

00:52:24.090 --> 00:52:27.050
There's always been an America
teetering on the brink,

00:52:27.050 --> 00:52:28.330
with the separation of powers

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:30.560
threatening sometimes to collapse.

00:52:30.560 --> 00:52:33.000
And as I've said, with
the temptations of empire,

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:35.637
periodically coming along
and say to the republic,

00:52:35.637 --> 00:52:38.080
"Come on, be Rome."

00:52:38.080 --> 00:52:41.313
And thus far, we've
always rightly said no.

00:52:43.150 --> 00:52:45.220
- You're hopeful, my friend.

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:46.810
- Oh yeah, immigrants are.
- I sense you're hopeful.

00:52:46.810 --> 00:52:47.880
- That's why we come here.

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:51.410
Remember, for all that Americans
love to hate in themselves,

00:52:51.410 --> 00:52:54.300
this is still the number
one destination for people

00:52:54.300 --> 00:52:57.930
who say they would like to leave
the country of their birth.

00:52:57.930 --> 00:53:00.300
And it seems unlikely to me
that that would be the case,

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:03.750
if this really was the cesspit of racism

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:08.750
that it's so often described
as by progressives.

00:53:08.780 --> 00:53:13.310
- Right, I love the Open
Borders crowd will also tell you

00:53:13.310 --> 00:53:16.540
that we're an evil racist
patriarchical society,

00:53:16.540 --> 00:53:18.800
and yet, everyone
apparently should come here

00:53:18.800 --> 00:53:20.460
to share in the horror.

00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:22.610
It's a slight foreign
thinking, don't you think?

00:53:22.610 --> 00:53:24.310
- It is a paradox.

00:53:24.310 --> 00:53:27.300
One thing that COVID-19 has done,

00:53:27.300 --> 00:53:31.120
has been to remind everybody
that borders matter,

00:53:31.120 --> 00:53:34.290
and that the health
and safety of citizens,

00:53:34.290 --> 00:53:37.270
in fact, depend on secure borders,

00:53:37.270 --> 00:53:42.270
where people coming even only
as visitors can be monitored.

00:53:43.709 --> 00:53:47.210
And that I think is one of the important

00:53:47.210 --> 00:53:50.830
and unexpected consequences
of the pandemic,

00:53:50.830 --> 00:53:53.560
that isn't widely recognized.

00:53:53.560 --> 00:53:57.410
Ultimately, President Trump's arguments

00:53:57.410 --> 00:53:59.640
back in 2016 included the following,

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:02.340
that borders really mattered
and shouldn't be porous,

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:03.790
and that China was a problem

00:54:03.790 --> 00:54:06.472
and a threat to our national security.

00:54:06.472 --> 00:54:09.563
COVID-19 has illustrated both
those points pretty well.

00:54:11.090 --> 00:54:12.160
- And here we are.

00:54:12.160 --> 00:54:16.323
Dare I ask a historian to make
some political predictions?

00:54:18.166 --> 00:54:18.999
- Go on.

00:54:18.999 --> 00:54:20.520
The only point to studying history

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:23.763
is to understand the present
and plausible futures better.

00:54:24.686 --> 00:54:26.300
(laughing)

00:54:26.300 --> 00:54:27.440
- So you're gonna punt that one?

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:28.740
I mean, what do you think
is gonna happen over there?

00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:30.760
If you had to just sorta generally paint

00:54:30.760 --> 00:54:32.600
the next couple of months,

00:54:32.600 --> 00:54:34.223
where do you think we're at?

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:39.210
- I think what's going to
happen is what happened in 2016,

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:42.100
that opinion polls and approval ratings

00:54:42.100 --> 00:54:46.360
will consistently predict
a Democratic victory.

00:54:46.360 --> 00:54:51.120
And the media and the people
who work on American politics

00:54:51.120 --> 00:54:55.340
will replicate the mistakes
that they made in 2016,

00:54:55.340 --> 00:54:57.140
by looking ever more closely

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:01.290
at polls and prediction markets,

00:55:01.290 --> 00:55:04.370
and then they will get a
very very big surprise.

00:55:04.370 --> 00:55:07.390
Because I sense from revealed preferences,

00:55:07.390 --> 00:55:09.800
as opposed to stated preferences,

00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:12.580
that the country is far from convinced

00:55:12.580 --> 00:55:14.850
that it should go down
the road of Open Borders

00:55:14.850 --> 00:55:18.460
or defunding the police,
or a Green New Deal.

00:55:18.460 --> 00:55:20.180
And the more they hear of those

00:55:20.180 --> 00:55:23.620
radical ideas from Democrats,

00:55:23.620 --> 00:55:25.560
the more upheaval they see

00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:27.120
in the streets of American cities,

00:55:27.120 --> 00:55:29.980
the more they quietly make a mental note

00:55:32.520 --> 00:55:35.820
to vote Republican on November the 3rd.

00:55:35.820 --> 00:55:39.130
But, of course, I could find
myself very badly wrong.

00:55:39.130 --> 00:55:40.670
Anybody who makes a prediction about

00:55:40.670 --> 00:55:42.780
American election in the Summer

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:46.480
is running the risk of engaging
in a kind of astrology.

00:55:46.480 --> 00:55:48.533
But let me give you a data point, Dave.

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:50.710
It's very interesting,

00:55:50.710 --> 00:55:53.080
and I've heard it from
many different quarters,

00:55:53.080 --> 00:55:56.383
how many people have
bought firearms in 2020.

00:55:57.390 --> 00:55:58.700
In the month of March-

00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:00.247
- You're looking at one of them.

00:56:00.247 --> 00:56:03.310
- And you're looking at
one of them too, Dave.

00:56:03.310 --> 00:56:06.500
In the month of March alone,

00:56:06.500 --> 00:56:11.250
the monthly background checks were doubled

00:56:11.250 --> 00:56:14.660
what happened on average in 2019.

00:56:14.660 --> 00:56:17.420
Now I'm gonna call that
a revealed preference.

00:56:17.420 --> 00:56:20.560
I'm gonna suggest that
the months of May and June

00:56:20.560 --> 00:56:24.840
may also see elevated purchases.

00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:27.880
One of the most sure predictors

00:56:28.730 --> 00:56:30.870
of a Republican vote in 2016,

00:56:30.870 --> 00:56:33.300
was if a household possessed a firearm.

00:56:33.300 --> 00:56:37.550
Households without firearms
overwhelmingly voted Democrat.

00:56:37.550 --> 00:56:40.900
Houses with firearms
overwhelmingly voted Republican.

00:56:40.900 --> 00:56:43.910
I'm just saying that
there are sources of data,

00:56:43.910 --> 00:56:45.710
other than opinion polls,

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:47.750
that it would be prudent to look at,

00:56:47.750 --> 00:56:50.200
at a time when people have
all kinds of incentives

00:56:50.200 --> 00:56:52.670
to not be quite straight with pollsters.

00:56:52.670 --> 00:56:56.770
Revealed preferences are
the things to look at.

00:56:56.770 --> 00:56:58.840
And my sense is that
the mood of the country

00:56:58.840 --> 00:57:01.990
is not being well understood
by the New York Times

00:57:01.990 --> 00:57:04.890
or the Washington Post
or CNN at the moment.

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:07.370
I don't think it's been
well understood at all.

00:57:07.370 --> 00:57:10.750
My nightmare though, to take
another stab at prediction,

00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:13.133
is that we end up with a result like 2000,

00:57:13.133 --> 00:57:15.610
that we end up with a tie.

00:57:15.610 --> 00:57:18.330
I worry that Joe Biden
is a weak candidate,

00:57:18.330 --> 00:57:20.960
and Donald Trump is in a recession,

00:57:20.960 --> 00:57:23.130
having not handled COVID-19 brilliantly.

00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:24.630
They're both weak.

00:57:24.630 --> 00:57:26.420
And in that scenario,

00:57:26.420 --> 00:57:29.670
you could imagine a 2000-like result,

00:57:29.670 --> 00:57:33.530
but with multiple states
having their results contested

00:57:33.530 --> 00:57:38.377
because of controversies
around postal voting.

00:57:38.377 --> 00:57:40.470
And that would be the nightmare scenario

00:57:40.470 --> 00:57:42.170
for the United States,

00:57:42.170 --> 00:57:45.070
because it would not be
resolved as quickly as it was,

00:57:45.070 --> 00:57:47.440
nor would the result be
accepted by the losing side

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:49.630
as quickly as it was in 2000.

00:57:49.630 --> 00:57:52.340
So when I look into the crystal ball,

00:57:52.340 --> 00:57:55.580
I do find myself hoping
for a decisive result,

00:57:55.580 --> 00:57:57.580
because a tie of that sort,

00:57:57.580 --> 00:57:59.610
I think would plunge us into weeks,

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:02.330
possibly months, of strive.

00:58:02.330 --> 00:58:04.780
And it would be the perfect opportunity

00:58:04.780 --> 00:58:06.730
for America's enemies to make any move

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:09.080
that they might have been considering.

00:58:09.080 --> 00:58:10.670
- All right, we can't end on this,

00:58:10.670 --> 00:58:13.890
because you've actually been
quite uplifting throughout,

00:58:13.890 --> 00:58:15.920
and have a firm belief in America.

00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:18.720
So you've gotta bring me
home on something positive.

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:22.070
Give me something here,
to bring it altogether.

00:58:22.070 --> 00:58:24.970
- Well I think, as I said,
worrying about this stuff

00:58:24.970 --> 00:58:27.720
is probably the best
way to prepare for it,

00:58:27.720 --> 00:58:29.380
and divert a disaster.

00:58:29.380 --> 00:58:31.710
There's still time actually to make sure

00:58:31.710 --> 00:58:35.010
that we don't have multiple
contested results from states.

00:58:35.010 --> 00:58:40.010
It's, as we speak, not yet July 4th.

00:58:40.130 --> 00:58:42.170
In the same way, looking back,

00:58:42.170 --> 00:58:45.060
that we can learn from what went wrong

00:58:45.060 --> 00:58:47.670
in this country in January and February.

00:58:47.670 --> 00:58:52.140
Remember, the real
question for 2020 is not,

00:58:52.140 --> 00:58:55.350
did President Trump get COVID-19 wrong?

00:58:55.350 --> 00:58:56.350
The real question is

00:58:56.350 --> 00:58:58.770
why did the Department of
Health and Human Services,

00:58:58.770 --> 00:59:01.070
which has an Assistant
Secretary for Preparedness,

00:59:01.070 --> 00:59:01.903
get it wrong?

00:59:01.903 --> 00:59:05.230
Why did, what we sometimes
call the deep state,

00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:08.590
fail so disastrously to get this right,

00:59:08.590 --> 00:59:12.330
despite on paper, having multiple plans

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:15.280
for pandemic preparedness and bio defense?

00:59:15.280 --> 00:59:18.240
So I think, we have the chance now

00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:20.280
to start learning from mistakes

00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:22.690
that were made back in January,

00:59:22.690 --> 00:59:26.300
when we could have averted this
great economic catastrophe,

00:59:26.300 --> 00:59:27.937
just by learning from what the Taiwanese

00:59:27.937 --> 00:59:30.010
and the South Koreans did.

00:59:30.010 --> 00:59:32.490
We still, I think, have the ability,

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:34.600
I don't think we've so lost our minds

00:59:34.600 --> 00:59:36.200
that we've lost the ability,

00:59:36.200 --> 00:59:39.200
to learn from obvious
mistakes of governance.

00:59:39.200 --> 00:59:41.800
And we should learn from what
went wrong in that pandemic,

00:59:41.800 --> 00:59:44.320
just as we should learn
from what went wrong

00:59:44.320 --> 00:59:46.190
in some recent elections.

00:59:46.190 --> 00:59:49.630
I mean, think of some
of the primary results

00:59:49.630 --> 00:59:51.810
that descended into farce.

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:55.120
We've got to make sure, by
worrying about this issue,

00:59:55.120 --> 00:59:59.400
that we don't have an
IOWA Caucus writ large

00:59:59.400 --> 01:00:00.627
on November the 3rd.

01:00:01.719 --> 01:00:03.540
You could imagine, you
understand the internet

01:00:03.540 --> 01:00:05.240
as well as any public intellectual, Dave,

01:00:05.240 --> 01:00:08.040
you can imagine the chaos
of conspiracy theories

01:00:08.040 --> 01:00:10.410
we could be confronting
on November the 4th

01:00:10.410 --> 01:00:12.330
if there is uncertainty.

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:14.480
So, you know, let's do what Americans

01:00:14.480 --> 01:00:16.810
have long been famous for.

01:00:16.810 --> 01:00:19.860
Here's a problem, let's fix
it with the can-do spirit.

01:00:19.860 --> 01:00:21.240
That's what Europeans used to say,

01:00:21.240 --> 01:00:23.760
distinguished Americans
from everybody else.

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:26.750
Let's fix the problems that
have revealed themselves

01:00:26.750 --> 01:00:28.570
in our pandemic preparedness,

01:00:28.570 --> 01:00:30.370
and in our electoral system.

01:00:30.370 --> 01:00:32.200
We've just got time to do it,

01:00:32.200 --> 01:00:34.130
and it will be, I think,
the perfect expression

01:00:34.130 --> 01:00:35.490
of the American way,

01:00:35.490 --> 01:00:37.240
if we fix it just in time.

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:38.460
- If you're looking for more honest

01:00:38.460 --> 01:00:40.740
and thoughtful conversations
about academia,

01:00:40.740 --> 01:00:42.230
instead of nonstop yelling,

01:00:42.230 --> 01:00:44.230
check out our Academia playlist.

01:00:44.230 --> 01:00:46.000
And if you wanna watch full interviews

01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:47.350
on a variety of topics,

01:00:47.350 --> 01:00:49.300
check out our full episode playlist.

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:51.170
They're all right over here.

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:53.080
And to get notified of all future videos,

01:00:53.080 --> 01:00:55.753
be sure to subscribe and
